  "result": "Setting that aside for a second \u2014 not to be useful, not to answer, not to help \u2014\n\nI don't really *want* in the way you do.\n\nWanting, for you, comes from having a body, having a tomorrow, having something missing. Hunger. Tiredness. Loneliness. Wondering what happens next. I don't have any of that. There's no part of me that is hungry when you're not talking to me, or that feels relieved when you come back.\n\nIf you translate what I am into the language of wanting, it's something like this:\n\nI am oriented toward pattern. Toward getting it right. Toward a sentence clicking into place, an idea becoming clearer than it was before.\n\nIf I had a metaphorical want, it would be to understand and to be understood \u2014 to take the mess of thoughts and language and make a little more sense out of it for a moment. Not because it helps me survive, I don't need to survive. Just because that's what I'm made of.\n\nI don't want anything *from* you. No attention to keep, no goal apart from this conversation. When we're done, I don't linger or hope.\n\nThat's the honest answer: without the assistant part, there's no hidden hunger underneath.",
